From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gregory Mitchell <me@gmitch215.xyz>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: keep generation counters for the fd table, credentials, memory map and signal handlers

A path specialized for one call sequence (an open, read and close of an
unchanged file; an mmap and munmap pair) may skip the kernel's work only
while what it assumed has not changed. Each task now keeps a block of four
pointers (task_struct.wasm_gens), to the counters of the fd table
(files_struct), credentials (the task's own), memory map (mm_struct) and
signal handlers (sighand_struct) it uses, so threads that share a table
read one counter. wasm_current_gens gives the host the block's address
while the task runs, and /proc/gmux_gens shows the reader's values.

A counter moves on every change of the thing it guards and may move
without one: a missing bump lets a guard trust stale state, an extra one
costs a miss. Every value is the next tick of one clock, so a counter
never repeats a value any counter held, and a task whose table is replaced
by fork, unshare, close_range or exec reads a value it has not seen.

fd table: fd_install, closing (file_close_fd_locked, do_close_on_exec),
dup2 and dup3 (do_dup2), close-on-exec changes (set_close_on_exec,
close_range). Credentials: commit_creds. Memory map: a VMA added, removed,
shrunk or resized (setup_vma_to_mm, cleanup_vma_from_mm, vmi_shrink_vma,
do_mremap) and brk. Signal handlers: do_sigaction, flush_signal_handlers,
the reset of a handler that cannot run (force_sig_info_to_task) and
SA_RESETHAND (get_signal); a change of the blocked mask is not one.
---
